      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum cascade laser is a distinct semiconductor-light-source invention that opened the mid-infrared and terahertz spectral regions to practical coherent sources.",
        "attribution": "Cho supplied crucial semiconductor heterostructure foundations, while Capasso and Faist are central to the original quantum-cascade concept and its experimental realization.",
        "maturity": "The principle has been repeatedly verified and developed into a broad instrument platform over decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relative boundary between enabling heterostructure technology and the original cascade-laser invention requires careful historical documentation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B018",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks have transformed precision measurement, making extraordinarily accurate and reproducible frequency standards into working experimental instruments.",
        "attribution": "Katori introduced the magic-wavelength lattice-clock approach; Ye made decisive experimental advances and established its exceptional metrological capability.",
        "maturity": "Independent laboratories have confirmed the performance and applications, including tests of fundamental physics and geodesy.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The division between the foundational clock architecture and later precision demonstrations should be specified precisely."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B002",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Masato Sagawa",
        "John J. Croat"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Neodymium-iron-boron magnets are a major materials invention with lasting, pervasive technological consequences in motors, electronics, and energy systems.",
        "attribution": "Sagawa and Croat independently established closely related Nd-Fe-B permanent-magnet materials, making their paired credit historically compelling.",
        "maturity": "The material class has been extensively characterized, manufactured, and indispensable in technologies for decades.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The exact framing must recognize the near-simultaneous independent routes without overstating identity of their contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry made quantum-wave interference of matter into an exceptionally sensitive measurement tool, with deep and practical consequences.",
        "attribution": "Bordé developed foundational atom-optical interferometry, Pritchard advanced precision atom methods, and Kasevich delivered landmark interferometric measurements.",
        "maturity": "The methods are experimentally robust, independently reproduced, and central to modern inertial and gravitational sensing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has several early contributors, so priority and the most prize-defining experimental milestones need close archival review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B040",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Eli Yablonovitch",
        "John D. Joannopoulos",
        "Sajeev John"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Photonic crystals established that structured dielectric media can control electromagnetic modes in a manner analogous to electronic crystals, opening a durable field and device platform.",
        "attribution": "John and Yablonovitch supplied seminal band-gap concepts, while Joannopoulos provided central theoretical foundations and enabled systematic design.",
        "maturity": "Photonic band gaps and associated devices have extensive experimental confirmation and long-standing influence.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The appropriate balance between conceptual originators, theory, and experimental realization requires careful checking."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jagadeesh S. Moodera",
        "Stuart S. P. Parkin",
        "Terunobu Miyazaki"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance turned spin-dependent tunnelling into a high-impact measurable and technological effect, especially for magnetic information storage.",
        "attribution": "Miyazaki and Moodera made decisive room-temperature observations, and Parkin made foundational contributions to exploiting magnetic multilayers and tunnel junctions technologically.",
        "maturity": "The effect is repeatedly confirmed and embedded in mature spintronic devices.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The relation of the room-temperature breakthrough to earlier tunnelling and giant-magnetoresistance work must be distinguished carefully."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B014",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jian-Wei Pan"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Multiphoton entanglement experiments and satellite quantum links made quantum communication a demonstrated capability at unprecedented scale.",
        "attribution": "Pan is a clearly central experimental leader in the listed advances, though the underlying field rests on a broad international foundation.",
        "maturity": "The central experimental claims have been reproduced and the satellite demonstrations provide direct, visible validation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "A one-person attribution may not adequately capture essential conceptual, instrumental, and mission-level contributors."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B026",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Yoshinori Tokura",
        "Sang-Wook Cheong",
        "Ramamoorthy Ramesh"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magnetoelectric multiferroics revealed a powerful coupling of electric and magnetic order and created a fertile experimental materials-physics field.",
        "attribution": "Cheong, Tokura, and Ramesh each made major contributions to discovering, understanding, and experimentally advancing multiferroic oxides.",
        "maturity": "The phenomena are well established through extensive structural, spectroscopic, and functional measurements.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is distributed across several material systems and contributors, making a sharply delimited prize citation difficult."
        ]
      }
    }
